{:edb/db-before-t 942 :edb/db-after-t 943 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534255 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:19:22.215Z" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416257 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416261 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416262 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416267 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416258 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416259 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416264 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416270 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416260 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416263 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416268 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416265 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416266 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416269 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186179842 1050 1080.0 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178269 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416239 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180100 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180100 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(2x + 1)m/s^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x = 1$ to $x = 2$." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180100 1074 "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $1.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=1.5\\cdot (2x+1)=(3x+1.5)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3x+1.5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}+2-({1}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(6-2) \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178275 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416249 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180101 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180101 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2tm/s^{2}$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t = 2s$." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180101 1074 "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)={2}^{2}=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178299 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416240 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180105 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{2}+t+1)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=0\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180105 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^{2} + t + 1)m$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 0s$ and $t = 2s$." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180105 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{2}+t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+1) \\\\  & =2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=0$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(0)=2(0)+1=1\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178305 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416255 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180106 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180106 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6xN$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v = 4m/s$ at $x = 1$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x = 2$." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180106 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-1) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\ 9 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $5\\,m/s.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178311 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416244 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180107 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180107 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3x^{2} + 1)N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v = 4m/s$ at $x = 1$, calculate the particle's initial velocity." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180107 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+1-0 \\\\  & =2\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =8-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180764 1064 "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180764 1064 "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186180766 1064 "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity." 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186180766 1064 "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFor example, let a particle [math]P[/math] of mass [math]5\\,\\rm{kg}[/math] is moving along the [math]x[/math]-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is [math]4t\\,\\rm{m/s},[/math] where [math]t \\geq 0[/math] is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times [math]t=1\\,\\rm{s}[/math] and [math]t=2\\,\\rm{s}.[/math]\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by [math]v(t)=4t,[/math] where [math]t[/math] is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at [math]t=1[/math], is\n\\[\nv_0 = v(1) = 4(1) = 4\\,\\rm{m/s},\n\\]\nand final velocity, at [math]t=2[/math], is\n\\[\nv_1 = v(2) = 4(2) = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nW\n&= \\Delta\\, \\text{KE} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (8)^2 - \\dfrac12 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (4)^2 \\\\[5pt]\n&= 160 - 40 \\\\[5pt]\n&= 120\\,\\rm{J}.\n\\end{align*}" 13194139534255 17592186045417 false] [17592186416143 1080 "6\\,J" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416143 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416143 1083 #uuid "016d8147-2b48-53a6-82af-fafe816ab49a" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416144 1080 "18\\,J" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416144 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416144 1083 #uuid "0c59d75c-f137-5ad0-adb1-d98444184b0b" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416145 1080 "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416145 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416145 1083 #uuid "0fd6aeb4-c566-5a88-9e32-388d6c8dfea3" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416146 1080 "20\\,J" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416146 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416146 1083 #uuid "112a9bb0-329a-50bc-862b-f1f1908e9ef2" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416147 1080 "\\frac{11}{2}\\,m/s" 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416147 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534255 17592186045417 true] [17592186416147 1083 #uuid "11a230f6-8610-57b2-a192-bbc685af3d61" 